@page "/guides/analyzers"

<PageTitle>Analyzers · Trailblazor</PageTitle>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Headline" As="TextElement.H1">Analyzers</TbText>
<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" class="docs-lead">
    The Trailblazor package includes an analyzer that reads your markup as the app builds. It warns, at the line in
    the <code>.razor</code> file, about a control or field screen readers can't name, a dialog with no title, and a
    value that snaps back after the user changes it.
</TbText>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Rules</TbText>
<TbTable aria-label="Analyzer rules">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th scope="col">Rule</th>
            <th scope="col">Warns when</th>
            <th scope="col">Fix</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <th scope="row">TB001</th>
            <td>
                A control that needs a name has none, or a blank one: an icon button, menu, popover, toolbar, toolbar
                button, chip group, toggle group, progress, tab, tree, submenu, option group, sortable list or insert
                divider.
            </td>
            <td>Set the parameter the warning names, such as <code>AriaLabel</code>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <th scope="row">TB002</th>
            <td>
                A <code>TbDialog</code>'s <code>Title</code>, or the title passed to <code>IDialogService</code>, is
                blank.
            </td>
            <td>Give the dialog a title. It names the dialog and is announced as the dialog opens.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <th scope="row">TB003</th>
            <td>
                An input is given a <code>Value</code> without <code>ValueChanged</code>, so the page's next render
                undoes the user's change.
            </td>
            <td>
                Use <code>@@bind-Value</code> or handle <code>ValueChanged</code>. A field can set
                <code>ReadOnly</code> instead.
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <th scope="row">TB004</th>
            <td>A field has no <code>Label</code> and no <code>AriaLabel</code>.</td>
            <td>Set <code>Label</code>, or <code>AriaLabel</code> when the layout has no room for one.</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</TbTable>
<CodeBlock Label="Search.razor" Code="@Mistakes" />
<CodeBlock Label="The build's warnings" Code="@Warnings" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">What it can see</TbText>
<TbText>
    The analyzer reads the values written in markup. A name held in a variable or worked out by an expression isn't
    known until the page runs, so the analyzer leaves it to the component. Most of the controls above check it when
    they render and throw if it's blank.
</TbText>
<TbText>
    An attribute written on a field goes on its wrapper, not its input, so it doesn't name the field. Put an
    <code>aria-label</code> in <code>InputAttributes</code> instead. A field with a native input, such as a text
    field or checkbox, can also take an <code>id</code> so a <code>TbLabel</code> elsewhere can name it; a select, list
    box or range can't be named that way. A radio or checkbox group is the exception: its attributes go on its
    fieldset, so <code>aria-label</code> or <code>aria-labelledby</code> written on it does name it. A tree can also be
    named by another element with <code>aria-labelledby</code>. Attributes spread with
    <code>@@attributes</code> may hold a name, so a component given them isn't checked. A required parameter that's
    left out, such as an icon button's <code>AriaLabel</code>, already gets Razor's own RZ2012 warning, so TB001 warns
    only when it is blank.
</TbText>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Turning a rule off</TbText>
<TbText>
    The rules are warnings, or errors in a project that treats warnings as errors. To turn one off for a whole
    project, list it in <code>NoWarn</code>.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="MyApp.csproj" Code="@NoWarn" />

@code {
    private const string Mistakes = """
        <TbIconButton Icon="Icons.Search" AriaLabel="" />
        <TbTextField @bind-Value="_query" />
        <TbCheckbox Label="Only open trails" Value="true" />
        """;

    private const string Warnings = """
        Search.razor(1,35): warning TB001: TbIconButton has no accessible name. Set AriaLabel.
        Search.razor(2,20): warning TB004: TbTextField has no label. Set Label, or AriaLabel when the layout has no
            room for one.
        Search.razor(3,38): warning TB003: TbCheckbox sets Value without ValueChanged, so the parent's next render
            undoes the user's change. Use @bind-Value, handle ValueChanged, or set ReadOnly.
        """;

    private const string NoWarn = """
        <PropertyGroup>
          <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);TB003</NoWarn>
        </PropertyGroup>
        """;
}
